  "summary": "Cold re-review of the All Saints homily (src/claude/liturgy/roman-rite/1962/propers/temporal/f02-all-saints: sections/homily/10-homily.tex, sections/homily/90-note.tex, homily.tex, format.tex) after the HOM-001 repair, against the homily profile, the stage fragment, guidance/editorial.md 'Say what the texts say, plainly', and the accepted study records. All ten REVIEW_INPUTS seals verified by SHA-256; the spoken body's seal is unchanged from review 0, confirming the body was untouched. The HOM-001 repair is confirmed at the sources, not on the repair record's word: the terminal note now routes 'if ye be Christ's, then are ye Abraham's seed' through Bede's Explanatio Apocalypsis I on 7:4 in E. Marshall's 1878 English (passage record 1-7-4.toml carries the clause verbatim), with the Douay wording given for contrast (the repository Douay corpus reads 'And if you be Christ's, then are you the seed of Abraham', matching the note's contrast exactly), and Gal 3:29 is gone from both the route paragraph's Douay list and the Douay References entry, with the Bede References entry and exact loci recording the citation. The two other changed files are truthful: generation-metadata.tex matches the packet's run header and its new derive-homily iteration-1 contribution describes the repair accurately (the 'packet-declared-effort=high' labels match the authoring packets' EFFORT lines; this xhigh review packet is an evaluator and writes no generation record), and research/production-review.md's iteration-1 account checks out at both sources it names, with every homily-preflight-0001 gate log in the run directory exiting 0. Independently re-verified a sample of the settled quotations at their repository payloads (Bede on Apoc 7:9 robes and palms; Chrysostom's golden chain, slave-beggar-stranger and all-in-anxiety clauses in NPNF I.10; Augustine's perfect standard, humble and God-fearing, 'This is the mass of the saints' and the 'Scripture has deceived me' objector in NPNF I.6 and I.8; the Douay wording of Apoc 7:9, Mt 11:28, Ps 33:10 and Mt 5:3, 8-10; the Lasance Introit, adapted Offertory, commemorated Collect 'trammels' and Postcommunion 'unceasing prayers' rows), plus the calendar arithmetic (1 November 2026 is a Sunday and the Twenty-third Sunday after Pentecost) and the word count and pace (my count 1,451 words against the note's 'about 1,445'; 11.1-12.0 minutes at 120-130 wpm holds). Read continuously as a listener, the unchanged body still speaks one intelligible exegetical argument from this proper's own texts, with one practicable response drawn from the commemorated Collect and said once, no questions, no proscribed devices, and no editorial matter in the speech; the note's loci and review facts are exact. Meaningful limits: I did not rebuild the PDF or re-time a delivery (none was claimed), and quotation checks beyond the repair and the sample above rest on review 0's verification of bytes that are unchanged under the seal. Verdict: PASS, no findings."
